WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.720
Temporary files are often used for uploads, transformations, exports,
reports, image processing, archive handling, and background jobs.

2
00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:18.942
They may feel less important because they are not intended to last, but
temporary files can contain sensitive data, collide with other processes,
persist unexpectedly, or be accessed by unintended users.

3
00:00:18.942 --> 00:00:22.121
They deserve the same security review as permanent files.

4
00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:33.084
Safe temporary file creation concepts include using APIs that avoid
predictable names, setting appropriate permissions, choosing the right
directory, and ensuring cleanup happens deliberately.

5
00:00:33.084 --> 00:00:39.246
Shared temporary directories require extra care because other users or
processes may operate in the same space.

6
00:00:39.246 --> 00:00:44.696
Predictable names and loose permissions can create confusion, collisions,
or unintended access.

7
00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:49.301
Concurrent workers and background jobs make lifecycle management more
important.

8
00:00:49.301 --> 00:00:53.907
A worker may create temporary files while processing several requests in
parallel.

9
00:00:53.907 --> 00:00:55.529
A job may fail before cleanup.

10
00:00:55.529 --> 00:00:58.643
A retry may encounter leftovers from a previous attempt.

11
00:00:58.643 --> 00:01:08.049
Cleanup should be observable enough that operators can detect
accumulation, and it should be safe enough that cleanup does not delete
files outside the intended work area.

12
00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:11.941
Working directory assumptions are another common source of mistakes.

13
00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:19.661
Python code may behave differently depending on where a process is
started, which container path is mounted, or which scheduler runs the job.

14
00:01:19.661 --> 00:01:25.823
File operations should use explicit, intended locations rather than
relying on the current working directory.

15
00:01:25.823 --> 00:01:32.440
Sensitive temporary data should not be left behind for debugging unless
there is a controlled retention and access plan.
